.TH SMTFIT 1
.SH NAME
smtfit \- every learner family under cross validation, its tables into a directory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smtfit
[
.B \-o
.I dir
]
[
.B \-r
.I seed
]
[
.B \-j
.I threads
]
[
.B \-m
.I places
]
[
.B \-t
.I seconds
]
[
.I dataset.tsv
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I smtfit
reads a dataset as
.IR smtdata (1)
writes one, from the file named or from the standard input, and puts the
questions of the project to it: does the shape of a formula tell
how long a solver takes over it, and whether it answers at all?
Three tasks are asked: the time, with a timeout at twice the limit; the time of the
instances the solver finished only; and the outcome among sat, unsat and timeout.
Each is asked under two splits of the rows into training, validation and test
parts, 70, 15 and 15 in a hundred:
one that keeps every family of benchmarks whole in a part, and one that deals the
rows out at random.
.PP
For each task and split, each family of learners is fitted over a grid of settings
by cross validation in five folds over the training rows, grouped by family under
the first split.
The setting with the least error on the time, or the greatest mean F1 on the outcome,
is then fitted over all the training rows and tried on validation and on test.
.PP
Before a fit, the features that never vary over the training rows and those that
copy another are dropped; the rest are taken as the logarithm of one plus the value,
and centered and scaled as the training rows are.
.PP
The families are
.B median
and
.BR majority ,
the guesses to beat;
.BR ridge ,
the plane of least squared error;
.BR logistic ,
one logistic fit a class against the rest;
.BR knn ,
the k nearest rows;
.BR bayes ,
a gaussian a class and feature;
.BR tree ,
a cart tree;
.BR forest ,
three hundred trees on bootstrap samples;
.BR boost ,
two hundred rounds of shallow trees fitted to what the rounds before left out;
and
.BR svm ,
a support vector machine over a radial kernel, by dual coordinate descent.
.PP
The fits run side by side on
.I threads
.RB ( \-j ,
one a processor by default);
no more than
.I places
of them
.RB ( \-m ,
a fifth of the threads by default)
hold a square matrix over their training rows at once.
The seed
.RB ( \-r ,
2026 by default)
decides the splits and every draw the fits make, so that a run repeats.
.PP
Into
.I dir
.RB ( \-o ,
.B out
by default)
go the tables:
.TP
.B cv.tsv
The score of every setting of every family under cross validation.
.TP
.B time.tsv
The chosen settings on validation and on test for the two tasks of time:
mean and root mean squared error, Spearman correlation and R squared, in the logarithm.
.TP
.B outcome.tsv
The chosen settings for the outcome: accuracy, mean F1, and the F1 of the timeouts.
.TP
.B conf.tsv
The confusion matrices on test.
.TP
.B pred.tsv
What each chosen setting predicted for every row of validation and of test.
.TP
.B split.tsv
The part every row fell in, task by task.
.PP
What the features are worth comes of
.IR smtimp (1),
over the tables here.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
The whole of it:
.RS
.nf
smtfit \-o out dataset.tsv
.fi
.RE
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Progress goes to the standard error, a line every two hundred fits.
A fit that panics brings the run down: a count that does not add up is worth nothing.
A run takes from three quarters of an hour to an hour and a quarter on twenty processors,
most of it in the support vector machines, which go four at a time to bound the memory.
